  "body": {
    "id": "lem:lgs-case-refinement",
    "kind": "lemma",
    "statement": "For every successful support-licensed execution of Lee--Ghassami--Shpitser Algorithms 1--2, the input-determined case function reaches a formula result.  At every existential line its canonical enumeration selects a witness whenever one exists; its returned factor kernels agree with the published kernels on \\(O_H(\\Theta_H)\\), independently of valid fixing order, and it never selects a context outside \\(H\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "InputI",
      "nextH",
      "SACSID",
      "H",
      "pH",
      "FH",
      "OH",
      "TH",
      "ThetaH",
      "posLedgerH"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "lem:lgs-algorithms-case-structure",
      "prop:input-determinacy",
      "lem:computed-closure-valid-sequence",
      "lem:context-construction-soundness",
      "lem:truncated-factorization-identity",
      "lem:computed-fixing-soundness",
      "lem:hybrid-kernel-soundness",
      "lem:district-assembly-soundness",
      "lem:nonresidual-progress",
      "def:sa-csid-machine"
    ],
    "proof_tex": "Proceed simultaneously over the finite published recursion tree and the machine work multiset.  Both start from the same LS-SWIG, ancestor set \\(Y^{*}\\), and canonically computed district partition.  Suppose corresponding district tasks have been reached.  The predicates defining \\(L(D^{*})\\), the reachable closure \\(C\\), the cases \\(C=D^{*}\\) and \\(S\\in C\\), and \\(\\operatorname{ch}^{*}(S)\\) are identical displayed predicates.  The closure lemma proves that the machine's finite enumeration returns the same unique closure and a valid sequence.  If the published successful branch has a supported context or Algorithm-2 triple satisfying its line predicate, that object occurs in the machine's finite list; therefore the canonical scan finds a passing object no later than exhaustion.  The machine cannot enter the associated failure branch.\n\nThe chosen witness need not be the first witness displayed in a paper derivation.  For a different valid fixing order with the same fixed set, \\(\\textnormal{lem:computed-fixing-soundness}\\) proves equality of the final kernel.  For two passing contexts or Algorithm-2 triples, \\(\\textnormal{lem:context-construction-soundness}\\) and \\(\\textnormal{lem:hybrid-kernel-soundness}\\) prove that both observable expressions equal the same normalized district kernel; after cross-multiplication their difference is zero modulo the retained constraints.  Thus the machine's canonical choice and the published choice agree on \\(O_H(\\Theta_H)\\).  The truncated factors agree by \\(\\textnormal{lem:truncated-factorization-identity}\\), and the joins agree by \\(\\textnormal{lem:district-assembly-soundness}\\).\n\nInduction over completed tasks now shows that every machine factor equals its published counterpart on the compatible image and that every ledger divisor is the actual divisor of that factor.  The successful recursion is finite, and \\(\\textnormal{lem:nonresidual-progress}\\) rules out diversion to a nonresidual stuck state.  Therefore the machine reaches a formula.  Every enumerated context lies in \\(H\\) by construction.",
